*.ilk

# Simulator artifacts
*.snap
*.replay
swtpcsim.log

# The assembled distribution. tools/build-package.sh writes dist/swtpcsim-<ver>-<target>/
# and the archive beside it -- an OUTPUT of the tree, never an input to it. The directory
# itself is TRACKED (via dist/README.md) so it always exists after a checkout: dist/ is
# also the release COLLECTION point (DISTRIBUTION.md 6), and a worker must be able to scp into
# it before the coordinator has built anything. Contents stay ignored; the README does not.
/dist/*
!/dist/README.md

# Binaries CI built, pulled down by tools/fetch-ci-binaries.sh. Scratch: overwritten on
# every fetch, read by nothing in the build or the tests.
/artifacts/

# The built documents. THE MARKDOWN IS THE SOURCE; the PDF is an artifact of it, of the
# binary (the reference chapters are printed from the program) and of a browser.
#
# The USER-FACING PDFs ARE TRACKED. tools/build-docs.sh makes docs/swtpcsim-manual.pdf and
# docs/swtpcsim-devguide.pdf, and they are versioned deliverables: they ship in the
# distribution zip beside the binary, and a fresh clone should carry the manual a reader can
# open without a pandoc-and-a-browser toolchain to build it. So they are committed on purpose
# (regenerate and re-commit them when the docs change), and NOT listed here.
#
# The line that is NOT ours to track is the SCANNED REFERENCE MANUALS in reference/ (below):
# vendor datasheets, tens of MB each, and burned into this repo's history twice already. That
# is the "no PDFs in the reference directory" rule -- our documentation is fine, their scans
# are not. The intermediate HTML the browser prints from stays an artifact too.
docs/*.html
.swtpcsim_history

# Disk/tape images are large and usually not ours to redistribute.
# Commit them deliberately with `git add -f` if a test genuinely needs one.
*.dsk
*.DSK
*.cas
*.img

# examples/ IS THE SHIPPED SET, and it is an allowlist: these are the examples the manual's
# quick start names, and the ONLY media that goes in the package -- one home each, with the
# tests pointed at them. The rules above would hide most of it (`*.DSK` swallows the disk
# image), so each shipped file is named. If you add one and git does not see it, that is this
# block doing its job -- add the line.
examples/**
!examples/**/
!examples/**/*.toml
!examples/**/*.ini
!examples/**/README.md
# The SWTPC FLEX 2.0 boot disk the swtpc quick start and the flex acceptance test name: a
# 40-track single-sided image (102,400 bytes, the DC-4's flex geometry). deramp.com
# provenance in docs/sources.md. The sibling FLEX2/3 images in the folder are NOT shipped.
!examples/flex/FLEX2-40.DSK
# The CP/68 boot disk the cp68 example names: TSC's CP/68 1.0, single-density 35x1x18x128
# (80,640 bytes, the DC-4's cp68 geometry). deramp.com provenance in docs/sources.md.
!examples/cp68/CP68.DSK
# The Altair 680b KCACR audio-cassette demo tape.
!examples/altair680/kcacr-demo.tap
# The debugger example's 6800 program -- the walkthrough's own material, not a disk image:
# the source for reading, the listing SYMBOLS LOAD reads, and the S-record LOAD reads.
# Assembled with the as0 6800 cross-assembler (docs/sources.md).
!examples/debugger/HELLO.ASM
!examples/debugger/HELLO.LST
!examples/debugger/HELLO.S19
# The lamp board the Developer Guide's "Writing a board" chapter builds. It is out of the
# shipping registry on purpose (the reader adds it), so it lives here rather than in src/ --
# but it is compiled into the test binary and driven by tests/test_lamp.cpp, which is the
# whole point: the chapter's code is a board that has to keep working, not a listing that can
# rot. So it must be tracked, header though it is.
!examples/boards/lamp/lamp.h
# Every example's README is rendered to a sibling README.pdf (tools/build-docs.sh, committed
# by CI) so a file manager with no Markdown viewer can read it, and so it travels in the
# package. One glob covers all of them, at any depth.
!examples/**/README.pdf

# Scanned manuals and datasheets. Same reasoning as the disk images, and then
# some: they are enormous (the 88-DCDD manual alone is 52 MB) and they are not
# ours. They are the SOURCE the boards were built from, not part of the build --
# keep them here on disk, cite them by filename in the code, and let
# docs/sources.md say where each one came from. See [altair-hardware-sources].
#
# This was decided once, and then a `git add -A` put 83 MB of them in history
# anyway; that history has been rewritten out. If you find yourself typing
# `git add -f reference/...`, you are about to do it a third time.
#
# The ONE exception, allowlist-style like disks/ above: the *.md files here are
# OURS -- distilled, text-only emulation references written FROM the scans, each
# citing its source PDF by filename. "Our documentation is fine, their scans are
# not." Those are tracked; the scans themselves stay ignored.
reference/*
!reference/*.md

# The `lineprinter` machine's capture file. `machines/lineprinter.toml` connects the
# 88-C700 to `file:printout.txt`, so merely running that machine from the repo root
# drops one here -- and an empty one got committed once by a `git add -A` (37afae6).
printout.txt

# The `parallel` machine's capture file, for the same reason: `machines/parallel.toml`
# connects the 88-PIO's `out` line to `file:parallel-out.txt`.
parallel-out.txt

# Agent scratch worktrees -- not repository content.
.claude/worktrees/

